  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 109 AM CDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nThunderstorms have been increasing in coverage over the past few \nhours, particularly along an axis over the Flint Hills. RAP \nmesoanalysis suggests this corresponds to the eastern edge of a zone \nof 700 mb WAA, which makes sense as forecast soundings suggest \nstorms will be rooted near 700 mb. Short term guidance keeps this \nWAA pattern going until early to mid morning tomorrow, which should \nin turn keep storms going through then. Despite moderate elevated \ninstability, weak effective shear has kept storms thus far sub-\nsevere. If moisture can locally deepen and storms become rooted \nlower to the ground, this could increase effective shear enough to \nallow for a few stronger storms with some wind/hail potential. The \nmain threat though should be locally heavy rainfall and isolated \nflash flooding. Areas of 2-3\", with very isolated higher pockets, \nseem plausible given a persistent focus of ascent for convection \nnear the Flint Hills. Once the WAA pattern weakens this morning, \nexpecting things to clear out by mid-day. Lingering clouds through \nthe afternoon may keep highs in the 80s, but if skies can clear \n(especially farther west) temperatures should heat into the low 90s.\n\nA warmer pattern will arrive for the weekend and the first half of \nnext week as ridging over the Four Corners region expands eastward. \nHowever a fast zonal jet with embedded shortwaves will remain in \nplace over the Northern Plains, keeping an east-west stationary \nboundary somewhere over Nebraska and Iowa. So we'll need to watch \nfor any convective systems to our north that can shove some outflow \nand/or cloud cover southward into our area. Nevertheless, low-level \nwinds stay mainly southerly, keeping sticky low/mid 70s dewpoints \naround. This should bring heat indices around or above 100 each day, \nas long as the aforementioned convection to our north doesn't play \ntoo large of a role.\n\nBy sometime mid-week, indications are for a stronger shortwave to \nonce again dig deeper troughing over the Great Lakes. This increases \nconfidence in some sort of cool-down. How much of a cool-down (near \naverage or below average) would be dependent on the amplitude of the \ntrough and its associated cold front. Rain and storm chances would \nalso increase along and north of wherever this front ends up.",
